Looking at the problem, I first check for answer leaks — the statement contains only the problem, no solution sketch or embedded answer. Proceeding to solve.

**Setup.** Let $I$ be the incenter, $r$ the inradius, and $\rho>r$ the radius of the drawn circle; put $k=\sqrt{\rho^{2}-r^{2}}$. Let $X,Y,Z$ be the touchpoints of the incircle with $BC,CA,AB$, and let $n_a,n_b,n_c$ be the unit vectors from $I$ to $X,Y,Z$ (so $X=rn_a$, etc.). Let $m_a,m_b,m_c$ be unit vectors along $BC,CA,AB$ oriented from $C\to B$, $C\to A$, $B\to A$. The circle $(I,\rho)$ meets each side in two points symmetric about the touchpoint at distance $k$:
$$D=rn_a+km_a,\quad P=rn_a-km_a,\qquad E=rn_b-km_b,\quad Q=rn_b+km_b,\qquad F=rn_c+km_c,\quad R=rn_c-km_c,$$
with signs matching the stated near/far conditions. Note $ID=IP=IE=IQ=IF=IR=\sqrt{r^{2}+k^{2}}=\rho$.

**Lemma.** Let two sides $\ell_1,\ell_2$ meet at a vertex $V$ (with $I$ inside the angle). Let $(I,\rho)$ meet $\ell_1$ at $A_1$ (far from $V$) and $A_2$ (near $V$), and $\ell_2$ at $B_1$ (near $V$) and $B_2$ (far from $V$). If $W=A_1B_1\cap A_2B_2$, then $I,A_1,A_2,W$ are concyclic.

*Proof of Lemma.* Use coordinates with $I$ at the origin and the internal bisector of $\angle V$ as the $x$-axis ($V$ on the positive axis). Write $c=\cos(V/2)$, $s=\sin(V/2)$. The sides are symmetric about the $x$-axis; with $n_1=(s,c)$, $n_2=(s,-c)$, $m_1=(c,-s)$, $m_2=(c,s)$:
$$A_1=(rs+kc,\,rc-ks),\quad A_2=(rs-kc,\,rc+ks),\quad B_1=(rs-kc,\,-rc-ks),\quad B_2=(rs+kc,\,-rc+ks).$$
Setting $A_1=(\alpha,\beta)$, $A_2=(\gamma,\delta)$ gives $B_2=(\alpha,-\beta)$ and $B_1=(\gamma,-\delta)$: the pairs $(A_1,B_2)$ and $(A_2,B_1)$ are mirror images across the $x$-axis. Hence the lines $A_1B_1$ and $A_2B_2$ are mirror images, so their intersection $W$ lies on the $x$-axis: $W=(w,0)$. From line $A_1B_1$,
$$w=\frac{\alpha\delta+\gamma\beta}{\beta+\delta},\qquad \alpha\delta+\gamma\beta=2sc(r^{2}+k^{2})=2sc\,\rho^{2},\qquad \beta+\delta=2rc,$$
so $w=\rho^{2}s/r$.

The circle through $I,A_1,A_2$ has its center on the perpendicular bisector of $A_1A_2$ (the line $rn_1+\mathbb{R}n_1$). Using $IA_1=IA_2=\rho$, its center is $O=\frac{\rho^{2}}{2r}\,n_1$ with radius $\frac{\rho^{2}}{2r}$ (it passes through $I$ since $|O|=\frac{\rho^{2}}{2r}$). The point $W=(w,0)$ lies on it iff
$$|W-O|^{2}=|O|^{2}\iff w^{2}-2w\cdot O=0\iff w=\frac{\rho^{2}}{r}s,$$
exactly the value found. Hence $W$ lies on the circle through $I,A_1,A_2$. $\square$

**Applying the Lemma.**
- At $C$ ($\ell_1=BC,\ell_2=CA$): $A_1=D,A_2=P,B_1=E,B_2=Q$, and $W=DE\cap PQ=U$. So $I,D,P,U$ are concyclic; $I$ lies on the circumcircle of $\triangle DPU$.
- At $B$ ($\ell_1=AB,\ell_2=BC$): the cross-intersection $FD\cap RP=T$, so $I,F,R,T$ are concyclic; $I$ lies on the circumcircle of $\triangle FRT$.
- At $A$ ($\ell_1=CA,\ell_2=AB$): the cross-intersection $EF\cap QR=S$, so $I,E,Q,S$ are concyclic; $I$ lies on the circumcircle of $\triangle EQS$.

Thus the incenter $I$ is a common point of all three circumcircles.

**Uniqueness.** Each circle has radius $R_0=\frac{\rho^{2}}{2r}$ (by the computation in the Lemma, depending only on $r,\rho$ and the symmetry of the two points on a side), with centers
$$O_a=R_0 n_a,\qquad O_b=R_0 n_b,\qquad O_c=R_0 n_c,$$
all lying on the circle centered at $I$ of radius $R_0$. Since $n_a,n_b,n_c$ are three distinct directions, $O_a,O_b,O_c$ are three distinct points on that circle, hence non-collinear; the three circles are distinct.

All three pass through $I$. The second intersection of the circles centered at $O_a$ and $O_b$ is the reflection of $I$ across the line $O_aO_b$ (their common chord is the perpendicular bisector of $O_aO_b$, which passes through $I$ since $|O_a|=|O_b|$; the two intersections are symmetric about $O_aO_b$). If the three circles shared a second common point $P\ne I$, then $P$ would be simultaneously the reflection of $I$ across $O_aO_b$, across $O_bO_c$, and across $O_cO_a$. But $I$ and $P$ have a unique perpendicular bisector, forcing these three lines to coincide, hence $O_a,O_b,O_c$ collinear — a contradiction. So no second common point exists.

Therefore the circumcircles of $\triangle FRT$, $\triangle DPU$, and $\triangle EQS$ have exactly one point in common, namely the incenter $I$.

